      "text": "First, multiply both sides by $x+2$ to get rid of the denominator:\n\n$$5(x+2) = x^3-2x^2-8x.$$\n\nExpanding the left side, we have:\n\n$$5x+10 = x^3-2x^2-8x.$$\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n$$x^3-2x^2-13x+10 = 0.$$\n\nThis is a cubic equation, which we can use the Rational Root Test to test for possible rational roots. The factors of 10 are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 5, \\pm 10$, so we test $x=1$:\n\n$$1^3-2(1)^2-13(1)+10 = -4 \\neq 0.$$\n\nWe test $x=-2$ again and find that it is not a root, as we want to include all possible $x$-values. So, we cannot use the Rational Root Test for finding the $x$-values that satisfy the equation, we need to evaluate it with Our next idea is to use factorization.\n\nWe know that $x+2$ is a factor of $x^3-2x^2-13x+10$, because it is common factor in the equation above. So, we can divide both sides of the equation by $x+2$.\n\nThe quotient will have a degree of 2 because the degree of the divisor is one more than the degree of the dividend.\n\n$$\\frac{x^3-2x^2-13x+10}{x+2} = \\frac{(x+2)(x^2-4x+5)}{x+2}$$\n\nWe can reduce this back to the original equation, except that it becomes:\n\n$$x^2-4x+5=0$$\n\nWe do not need to factor this, it is already factored completely. Using the Quadratic Formula, we get:\n\n$$x=\\frac{4\\pm\\sqrt{16-20}}{2}=$$\n\n$$x=\\frac{4\\pm 2i}{2} =\\frac{2\\pm i}{1}.$$\n\nSince we are looking for real values only, we will discard the complex roots. The final answer is \\boxed{0}.",
