      "text": "We start by isolating $x$ in both cases, which leads to the steps shown below.\n\nSolution:\nFirst, let's simplify the equation.\n$\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x}{x+2}= 5$\n$\\frac{x^3 - 2x^2 - 8x - 5x -10}{x+2} = 0$\n$x^3 - 2x^2 - 13x - 10 = 0$\n\nThen, let's use synthetic division to factor the polynomial.\n\nStep 1: Set up the table.\n$\\begin{array}{c|ccc}\n1 & 1 & -2 & -8 & -10 \\\\\n\\hline\n& & 1 & -1 & 3 \\\\\n& 1 & -1 & -9 & -7 \\\\\n& 1 & -1 & -9 & -7 \\\\\n& \\hline\n& 1 & -3 & -27\n\\end{array}$\n\nStep 2: Bring down the first coefficient.\nWe then put this number in the next column.\n\nStep 3: Multiply it by the divisor, 1.\nNext, we repeat this simple process by multiplying the number we just found by the divisor, and then putting the result in the next column.\n\nStep 4: Subtract it from the old number.\nTo finish off this step, we subtract the result we found in Step 3 from the previous number we found in the column.\n\nStep 5: Repeat Steps 2-4\nWe repeat Steps 2-4 until all of the numbers to the right of the tent are zero.\n\nStep 6: The final fraction is the entire remainder.\nSince the remainder is zero, we stop the division process.\n\nOur final format: $x = -5, -1,$ and $2$\n\nFinally, we find the sum of the x-values. $-5+-1+2= \\boxed{-4}.$. The answer is \\boxed{-4}.\n\nLet's break this down step by step:\n\n1. Simplify the given equation $$5 = \\frac{x^3-2x^2-8x}{x+2}$$\n\nOriginal: $$\\frac{x^3-2x^2-8x}{x+2}$$\n\nCase 1: \n\n$(x+2) \\Longleftrightarrow x$\n\n$$\\frac{(x+2) \\cdot x^2-x^2-8x}{x+2}$$\n\nOriginal equation:\n\n$$x^2-x-8x= 0\\Rightarrow x \\cdot (x-9)=0$$\n\nSo $x=0$ or $9$\n\nCase 2:\n\n$(x+2) \\Longleftrightarrow 0$\n\n$$\\frac{x^2-(2 \\cdot 0) -8 \\cdot 0}{x+2}$$\n\nOriginal equation:\n\n$$x^2-8=0\\Rightarrow x=\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}$$\n\n\nSince the latter two cases give x values different of zero, the only one who makes the equation valid remains given by the first one.\n\nThus, x= 9, $2\\sqrt{2}$ and 0, with the sum result in -4\n\n$-5+(-1)+2=-4$",
